messages:
sasha: heads-up, running into weird partial-JSON errors in the .NET SSE client when a token refresh happens mid-stream. messages are arriving as fragments and our parser throws. any quick patterns people use for backpressure + incremental parse in C#?

raj: ugh yeah, seen this. are you using ReadAsStreamAsync + StreamReader.ReadLineAsync loop, or reading raw bytes?

sasha: ReadAsStreamAsync + ReadAsync into a buffer, then we append to a StringBuilder and try to JsonDocument.Parse once we see a closing brace. brittle tho.

olivia: recommendation: treat stream as a sequence of 'events' (SSE style) and feed each event payload into an incremental parser. For .NET use Utf8JsonReader (System.Text.Json) which supports partial JSON across spans if you maintain state.

kevin: example pattern: read bytes into a Span<byte>, call Utf8JsonReader with isFinalBlock=false; when you hit incomplete token, keep the leftover bytes for next read. also keep a max buffer size to avoid OOM if inputs stall.

raj: also important: backpressure. if your consumer can't keep up, use a bounded Channel<T> (System.Threading.Channels) or BlockingCollection with bounded capacity. when channel is full, pause reads (or apply a short Task.Delay) and rely on HttpClient cancellation to avoid infinite buffering.

sasha: can you paste a tiny snippet for the Utf8JsonReader flow?

olivia: here's a tiny gist-style snippet (very simplified):

```
// read loop
var leftover = new byte[0];
while (!ct.IsCancellationRequested) {
  var bytesRead = await stream.ReadAsync(buffer, ct);
  if (bytesRead == 0) break;
  var combined = new byte[leftover.Length + bytesRead];
  Buffer.BlockCopy(leftover, 0, combined, 0, leftover.Length);
  Buffer.BlockCopy(buffer, 0, combined, leftover.Length, bytesRead);

  var reader = new Utf8JsonReader(combined, isFinalBlock: false, state);
  while (reader.Read()) {
    if (reader.TokenType == JsonTokenType.StartObject) {
      // try parse object if complete, otherwise rely on state
    }
  }

  // capture unread bytes: combined.Slice((int)reader.BytesConsumed)
  leftover = combined.AsSpan((int)reader.BytesConsumed).ToArray();
}
```

olivia: you need to persist the JsonReaderState between loops (the "state" above). If you get a JsonException that indicates incomplete data, stash the bytes and continue.

kevin: about auth race: if you refresh token mid-stream and re-open connection, you'll sometimes receive a final fragment from old connection and a new partial from fresh connection. tag each stream attempt with a monotonic stream id and drop fragments from stale ids.

raj: yep. also ensure Authorization header refresh is atomic: update a shared token provider and have the reconnect logic call it, rather than flipping headers on an active HttpRequestMessage. avoids sending mixed auth on same request.

sasha: good call. for backpressure: if Channel.Full then drop or signal client? we want graceful degradation, not OOM.

kevin: prefer bounded channels + a meter. when full, increment a counter (metrics) and either drop oldest (sliding window) or stop reading and send a metric to start shedding upstream traffic. probably not drop silently.

olivia: small tip: for SSE where messages are framed like `data: {...}\n\n`, parse by event boundary first. don't try to parse arbitrary byte boundaries into JSON—SSE framing simplifies things so you can parse per-event body.

sasha: we're using the Redwood streaming API which is newline-delimited events, not full SSE lines every time. sometimes a single server chunk contains multiple events.

raj: then parse events by looking for the delimiter (double-newline) and iterate events inside a buffer. only hand complete event bodies to Utf8JsonReader. that reduces partial-object complexity.

olivia: and for clients that need low latency, prefer incremental consumption: push complete events into channel immediately when parsed, let consumer ack processed events so you can tune capacity.

kevin: one more: HttpClient should be constructed once and reused. use `HttpCompletionOption.ResponseHeadersRead` so the content stream is available as it arrives. also honor cancellation tokens when doing ReadAsync.

sasha: awesome. I'll refactor to: 1) event-framing boundary scan, 2) bounded Channel with capacity 256, 3) Utf8JsonReader with preserved state for leftover bytes, 4) stream-id tagging for reconnects, 5) metrics on drops.

olivia: ping if you want a quick code review. :thumbsup:

